{
  "summary": "The round 3 restart-loop proposal has a fatal gap: Python caches imported modules in sys.modules, so creating a fresh PipelineApp() inside a while loop does NOT actually pick up changed Python code. The old module bytecode remains loaded. To get fresh code, you'd need to explicitly purge changed modules from sys.modules before re-instantiating — but this is fragile because pipeline modules have deep cross-imports (tui imports runner, boards, lock, etc.), and selectively evicting modules without breaking transitive references or module-level state (e.g., global loggers, cached configs) is exactly the importlib.reload() fragility that round 1 already dismissed. The restart loop also introduces a lock re-acquisition race: between _release_lock() at loop bottom and re-acquire at loop top, another process (a concurrent pipeline runner) could grab the TUI lock. More fundamentally, round 2's challenge remains unanswered by all proposals — the only users who benefit from auto-reloading Python code are developers actively modifying tui.py or pipeline modules, and for them Ctrl+C plus re-run is a 2-second workflow that guarantees clean state. The complexity-to-benefit ratio of any auto-reload mechanism is poor. If this feature is still pursued, the only correct approach is the supervisor/wrapper pattern (round 1's approach 1): a parent process watches for changes, sends SIGTERM to the child TUI (triggering Textual's graceful shutdown and lock release via signal handler), waits for exit, then spawns a fresh subprocess with a completely clean Python interpreter — no sys.modules tricks, no lock races, no stale state."
